{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-99055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-99055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-99055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65c87b557a02b36762833ca769aa10341f3b7ce2c24c9eaa2e72f5f890400368"} {"question" "q-99055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-99055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=1$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=5$"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-99055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ and $x=5$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\,35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-0.630\\,85$ and $x≈0.416\\,35.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are ${x}_{1}≈-0.630\\,85$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.416\\,35.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.630\\,85\\cdot 0.416\\,35≈-0.26\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65c87b557a02b36762833ca769aa10341f3b7ce2c24c9eaa2e72f5f890400368"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.26"] ["math" "-0.55"] ["math" "-0.98"] ["math" "-1.13"] ["math" "-1.43"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-127386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.26"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The critical point of the function $f(x)=(2x+1){e}^{x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1){e}^{x-2}) \\\\  & ={(2x+1)}^{′}{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){({e}^{x-2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =[2+(2x+1)]{e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =(2x+3){e}^{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3){e}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-2}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-2}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65c87b557a02b36762833ca769aa10341f3b7ce2c24c9eaa2e72f5f890400368"} {"question" "q-344608" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344608" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}+12x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-15x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-5x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=1$ and  $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65c87b557a02b36762833ca769aa10341f3b7ce2c24c9eaa2e72f5f890400368"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-344842" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65c87b557a02b36762833ca769aa10341f3b7ce2c24c9eaa2e72f5f890400368"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6c15d0cb85a779879ef2"} {"question" "q-345484" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006865/state.json" "file_sha256" "78baeac5552c49a17020de45430048adcdd4e025b28f6